@yuwata mentioned: 
> when new lease is obtained, then assign new address and then remove old one. Previously, we first drop the old one, and assign new one, even if new one is the same as the old one
It sounds like a fix addressing my issue. under "fix" I mentioned this change

@ssahani Thank you for the references. I guess 'the bad case' you mean is e.g. [Fig 265 in a reference](http://www.tcpipguide.com/free/t_DHCPLeaseRenewalandRebindingProcesses-2.htm).

I think the fix #12685 and the original issue here is not related to such situation. The issue here is 'good case', that is, server is alive and provide addresses.

I am not sure now but if there exists an issue in 'bad case', then it is another issue, and not relevant to this. And, regardless the PR, we need to fix such a situation. But, in another later PR.

@yuwata I can follow your argumentation and agree with it
@ssahani The issue I described happened in the "good case". I expect uninterrupted network connectivity solong communication between DHCP client and DHCP server stays intact.
I agree with you, @ssahani , that in case of disturbed communication and unability to renew DHCP lease, things become more complicated and leased address must be removed after the expiration. But I'm not familiar enougth with DHCP specs to participate in detailed discussion about exact moment of lease expiration. IHMO a successfully renewed address must stay available on the interface exactly for the lease time (in my case 1 hour) after renewal even if any subsequent communication between DHCP client and server is broken. But again, it's just an opinion without RFC ref.

